model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "Runtime readiness admits new work only with a current health lease. Lease expiry or an unhealthy probe preserves dispatched HTTP and WebSocket streams, blocks admission and keeps one bounded renewal watcher running. A fresh healthy probe restores admission. A proven boot change closes the old streams. Membership invalidation and device revocation retain their independent immediate aborts. Health transition logs contain a closed reason, preserved or closed action, bounded remaining lease and probe timing; they never contain response bodies or exception messages."
      constraint "Membership renewal failures preserve only the last acknowledged clean revision until its original expiry plus 24 hours. Failed reads and installs never advance that deadline. Signed invalidation persists a newer dirty revision and aborts that pool before acknowledging a membership writer. Observed account or native-authority changes also persist a veto before fallible runtime repair. An actual runtime membership mismatch persists dirty state and aborts active use before policy installation, including a rejected dirty installation. Pool stream leases register before authority reads and assertion signing, so an in-flight admission cannot miss a concurrent veto. Failed admission releases its lease. A failed first installation, a different revision, dirty state or an elapsed grace deadline never grants model use. Controller reactivation preserves the acknowledged deadline; retained records without an acknowledged grace deadline keep their original lease. Pool views may use this same acknowledged authority while current identity and organization checks remain required."
    }
  }
  extend basalt.poolRuntime {
    metadata {
      constraint "Native policy installation still requires an authenticated signed projection with at most 15 seconds remaining. Only successful clean installation retains the exact account set for at most 24 hours beyond that signed expiry. Rejected installation and expired-envelope replay cannot extend it. A newer dirty policy immediately replaces the clean snapshot and has no grace. Request assertions, candidate-set vetoes and the independent scheduler-plugin health lease remain mandatory. An empty or replaced runtime never invents a previously installed policy. A model dispatch rides the endpoint's own still-current readiness proof for that boot, the one its answer to the controller's health read granted, instead of reading readiness a second time. The identity record and a running container are checked right before every send, a spent proof reads readiness again, and a launch or boot mismatch revokes the proof; a readiness refusal seen by another read does not, so a request admitted on the proof may still be sent within its lease. A readiness read that fails in transport or returns an unparsable body refuses as unhealthy readiness; any other failure stays a defect. With the fleet enabled and no sibling, a restarted primary may take requests before recovery's closing steps finish, because that boot was health-checked and given every current policy before the switch; a newer policy revision stays dirty until installed, and a dirty policy refuses admission, so no request is served on a boot without the current revision."
    }
  }
}
